 intro:'This lesson takes the calmest color in the deck and gives it two homes: the enormous one overhead and the small ones in the sock drawer.',
 what:'Blue is a color — the color of sky and sea — and this card is all blue, so the word and the color arrive together with no rival in sight.',
 mean:'Blue and purple spend months swapping places at two. That confusion is normal sorting work, and side-by-side comparison settles it faster than any explanation.',
 know:'Look up first: the sky is the biggest blue object your toddler will ever own. Then shrink the word down to socks and cups indoors — same word, two scales.',
 why:'Blue is everywhere once you start looking, which makes it the second-easiest color after red. Two colors in hand, the deck starts to feel like a game.',
 dev:'Color naming at two scales (vast sky, small sock), visual discrimination between near neighbors, and outdoor-to-indoor word transfer.',
 vocab:'blue, sky, sea, find, calm, cold',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say blue, then lift the card towards the ceiling: the sky is blue too, high, high up.\n2) Hunt blue indoors — two finds is a complete round: a blue cup, a blue book, a blue mitten if you own one.\n3) Put the blue card and the purple card side by side and let your toddler compare; eyes sort faster than explanations.\n4) When blue and purple swap places in their speech, say the right word once as you point — no quiz, no correction, just the model.\n5) Make blue a daily check: sky report at the window each morning, one blue find at breakfast, blue pyjamas at night if the drawer cooperates.',
 say:'"Blue! Look up — the sky is blue today. Can you find something blue in this room? A blue cup! Say it with me: blue. Blue like the sky, blue like the sea."',
 notice:'This card is the blue card — one color from edge to edge, with the word blue printed for toddlers. Ask your child to touch the color and say the word with you.',
 ask:'"What color is the sky?" "Can you find something blue in the room?" "Which card is the blue one?" "Is this blue or purple?"',
 childSay:'"Boo!" or "bue!" aimed first at the sky, then at everything below it. Some toddlers will report every cloud as not-blue — that is comparing, and comparing is the lesson.',
 real:'The morning sky is the free daily repetition; puddles, cars and jeans keep the indoors stocked. Blue is the color your toddler will spot before you do within the week.',
 play:'Sky report: a two-second weather check at the window every morning — is the sky blue today? A thumbs-up and the word blue is the whole ritual, and it runs on nothing.',
