 mean:'Two beats make the word bounce — yeh-low — and lellow is a normal, useful first draft. Keep the bounce; the consonants will land on their own.',
 know:'Food teaches colors faster than anything printed: hold up the banana, say the word, take the bite. Three repetitions at snack time outperform ten at the table with cards.',
 why:'Yellow hides in plain sight all day — ducks, sun, cheese, trucks. A color with a food anchor gets a guaranteed daily rehearsal slot.',
 dev:'Color naming through taste and smell (multisensory beats visual alone), two-syllable word practice, and noticing-brightness language.',
 vocab:'yellow, sun, banana, bright, duck, snack',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say yellow — then hold up a real banana beside it: same color, one word.\n2) Run a snack-time yellow hunt: banana, sweetcorn, a square of cheese. Name the color as each one lands on the plate.\n3) Hunt yellow around the house afterwards — the duck in the bath, a yellow toy, a flower on the walk.\n4) When lellow arrives, echo yellow clearly once and hand over the banana; the correction is your pronunciation, never their feelings.\n5) Keep one yellow food in the snack rotation all week so the word has a standing appointment every day.',
 say:'"Yellow! Bright yellow, like the sun. Look — a banana! Is it yellow? Yes! Can you say yellow? Yeh-low. What else can we find that is yellow?"',
 notice:'Yellow owns this card from edge to edge, with the word yellow printed underneath. Ask your child to say the word while laying a hand flat on the color.',
 ask:'"What color is this card?" "What color is your banana?" "Can you find something yellow in the bath?" "Is the sun yellow?"',
 childSay:'"Lellow!" with a banana held aloft, or a pointing finger at every duck within range. Expect the bath to become a yellow classroom for a week — rubber ducks are enthusiastic assistants.',
 real:'Bananas, corn on the cob, dandelions, school buses and the sun itself — yellow keeps appointments all day. The food version has the advantage of being eatable.',
 play:'Sunshine picnic: lay a yellow cloth or tea towel on the floor and fill it only with yellow snacks and toys. Everything on the blanket must earn its place by being yellow.',
 check:'Ask what color the card is, then what color the banana is. Two yellows — or two lellows — passes completely. A spontaneous yellow! at the fruit bowl ends the assessment early.',
 tips:'Snack-time teaching beats table teaching every time at two — appetite opens ears. Keep saying the full word even when lellow is adorable; the two-beat bounce is what they are practising.',
